<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gloucester</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Glevum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gloucester is a cathedral city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">River Severn</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It began as the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress and colonia of Glevum, fell to the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> after 577 and became a centre of the Hwicce and of Mercia. Its abbey, now the cathedral, holds the tomb of Edward II, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday survey</a> there in 1085.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gloucester.jpg" alt="Gloucester" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saffron Blaze · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gloucester_Docks_at_Night.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gloucester lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">River Severn</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotswolds">Cotswolds</a> to the east and the Forest of Dean to the west, at the traditional lowest bridging point of the river. The Roman settlement stood at an important crossing of the Severn near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fosse-way">Fosse Way</a>, the early front line after the Roman invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The Roman name Glevum came from a Brythonic word meaning bright, and the Welsh name Caerloyw, attested from the 8th century, means bright fort.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Glevum was founded around 48 as a fort at Kingsholm. Twenty years later a larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress was built on slightly higher ground, centred on the present Gloucester Cross, and a civilian settlement grew around it. Legio XX Valeria Victrix was probably based there until 66, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-ii-augusta">Legio II Augusta</a> after it, as Roman forces prepared to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. In 97, in the reign of Nerva, Glevum became a colonia, and it was probably the provincial capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britannia-prima">Britannia Prima</a>.</p><p>After the Roman withdrawal about 410, leading families of the Dobunni may have regained power. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> shows Gloucester as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dyrham">Battle of Deorham</a> in 577. It later belonged to the minor kingdom of the Hwicce, which became a sub-kingdom of Mercia after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirencester">Cirencester</a> in 628 and lost its separate standing from about 780. Mercia submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>'s Wessex in about 877–883.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-fitzhamon">Robert Fitzhamon</a> the first baron of Gloucester, linking the city's history closely with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a>. Henry II granted the first known borough charter in 1155, and in 1216 Henry III, aged nine, was crowned with a gilded iron ring in the Chapter House of the cathedral. The city withstood a Royalist siege in 1643 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-english-civil-war">First English Civil War</a>. John Hooper, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Gloucester, was burned there in 1555, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> made Gloucester a port in 1580.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gloucester is a cathedral city and the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>, with a population of around 132,000 including suburban areas. Gloucester Cathedral grew from an abbey dedicated to Saint Peter and holds the tomb of Edward II. The Gloucester and Sharpness Canal links the docks to the Severn Estuary. In 1780 Robert Raikes founded the first Sunday school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> there. The city has a long history in aircraft making, and the Gloster E.28/39, the first British jet aircraft, powered by Frank Whittle's turbojet, first flew at the company's airfield at Brockworth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gloucester” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ceawlin, who ruled in the later sixth century and was listed by Bede among the kings who held authority over the southern English, expanded westwards; the Chronicle records his victory at the Battle of Dyrham in 577, which brought <b>Gloucester</b>, Cirencester and Bath under West Saxon control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Most people lived in farmsteads and villages much like those of the Iron Age, but a network of towns grew up: coloniae of veterans at Colchester, Lincoln and <b>Gloucester</b>, later York, and civitas capitals for the native peoples, such as Verulamium (St Albans), Corinium (Cirencester) and Viroconium (Wroxeter).</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Howells said he wrote it after watching railway trucks shunting on the line below a cottage window in <b>Gloucester</b>, and dedicated it to his mother; its final cadence is much admired, and his fellow composer Patrick Hadley reportedly told him he would like to die to it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/es-ist-ein-ros-entsprungen">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</a></li><li><blockquote>Brackett lived at the Sabbathday Lake Shaker Village in New <b>Gloucester</b> before he was called to serve in the Ministry in 1848; for the next decade he served the community at Alfred, and later returned to New Gloucester.</blockquote><a href="/songs/simple-gifts">Simple Gifts</a></li><li><blockquote>Richard, Duke of <b>Gloucester</b>, was crowned after his nephew Edward V was declared illegitimate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-6">6 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Rout of Winchester, where Robert of <b>Gloucester</b>, chief supporter of Empress Matilda, was captured, the two sides exchanged him for King Stephen, whom Matilda's forces had held.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-1">1 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
